<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/955">955</a>–<a href="/years/983">983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> from 973, son of Otto I and husband of the Byzantine princess Theophanu. In 976 he gave the march on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> to Leopold of Babenberg, the beginning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and the French and was defeated by the Saracens at Stilo in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 982, dying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-ii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister des Registrum Gregorii · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_II_(HRE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto II was born in 955, the third son of Otto the Great and his second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-italy">Adelaide of Italy</a>. By 957 his older brothers Henry and Bruno had died, as had Liudolf, his father's son by his first wife, and the two-year-old Otto became heir apparent. His father entrusted his literary and cultural education to Archbishop William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, while Odo, margrave of the Eastern March, taught him the art of war and the kingdom's legal customs. He was made joint ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 961. On 25 December 967 Pope John XIII crowned him co-emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a step that was a prerequisite for his marriage to the Byzantine princess Theophanu, whom he married on 14 April 972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Otto the Great died in 973, and the 18-year-old Otto II succeeded peacefully. The nobles assembled on 8 May and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a>, elected him. During his first seven years he was constantly occupied with internal rivals and external enemies. His cousin Duke Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> came into conflict with him over the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, and Otto invested Henry's candidate as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> on 22 September 973 rather than risk civil war. In 974 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> rebelled and crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with Norwegian support, but Otto countered him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a> when his Norwegian allies sailed home.</p><p> After a revolt of other Ottonians he excluded the Bavarian line from the imperial succession. In the west he granted the sons of Reginar III their confiscated inheritance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and in 976 appointed his cousin Charles as Duke of Lower Lorraine.</p><p>From 980 Otto turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, supporting Pope Benedict VII and planning to annex the whole of the south, under the influence of Theophanu. This meant war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the Muslim Fatimid Caliphate. His campaigns ended in 982 with a disastrous defeat at Stilo. A rising of the Lutici Slavs followed in 983, which destroyed the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelberg">Havelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and forced the Empire to abandon its holdings east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto II died suddenly in his palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 7 December 983, aged 28, after a malaria outbreak had halted his preparations to resume the campaign in the south. He was buried in the atrium of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a>, the only Holy Roman emperor so buried. His three-year-old son Otto III succeeded him, which plunged the Empire into political crisis. Together with Theophanu he had founded the Benedictine Memleben Abbey, which became one of the richest imperial abbeys.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 980 <b>Otto II</b> issued a diploma confirming the immunity of the church of Liège, removing its lands from the jurisdiction of royal counts, and in 985 Otto III granted Notger the county of Huy, the first of many counties acquired by the see.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 976 <b>Emperor Otto II</b> separated Carinthia from the Duchy of Bavaria to weaken the rebellious Duke Henry the Quarrelsome, creating the first new duchy of the Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-carinthia">Duchy of Carinthia</a></li><li><blockquote>The abbesses Mathilde, a granddaughter of Otto I, and Theophanu, granddaughter of <b>Otto II</b>, made Essen a centre of Ottonian art; the Golden Madonna of about 980 and the Otto-Mathilde Cross belong to its treasury.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-essen">Imperial Abbey of Essen</a></li><li><blockquote>In 976 <b>Otto II</b> gave it to Leopold of Babenberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-austria">Margraviate of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>In 982 <b>Emperor Otto II</b> granted the bishop extensive rights over the city, and later Ottonian and Salian grants added counties and estates in Alsace and the Ortenau, making the bishops princes of the Holy Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-strasbourg">Prince-Bishopric of Strasbourg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Duchy of Saxony. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=973" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=973">What was here in 973 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
